  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 130 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances peak Thursday night into Friday \nmorning across much of west Texas and southeast New Mexico as the \nupper-level disturbance begins to advance across the area. By Friday \nevening/overnight, much of this activity departs to the north of our \nregion. Throughout the event, the best rain chances are still over \nthe eastern half of the area, specifically the Permian Basin and \nLower Trans-Pecos, where chances generally range between 80-90%. \nThat being said, measurable rainfall chances across the whole area \nlargely range from 60-80%. Some uncertainty still exists within \nguidance as to how much rain will fall. The highest chances for any \ngiven location seeing 1-2 inches of rain during the event continues \nto show up over the Lower Trans-Pecos and much of the Permian Basin \n(especially eastern and northern portions of the Basin). In general, \nensembles depict lower rainfall totals for western/southwesternmost \ncounties. Some models also depict the potential for a dry slot to\ndevelop as the system moves across the area, though where the \nassociated localized rainfall minima may set up remains uncertain \nat this time. In any case, flash flooding is going to be our main \nconcern throughout this timeframe for much of the region.\n\nAside from isolated remnant activity Saturday, most locations stay \ndry for Halloween as the system departs. By Sunday, another system \nnears the region from the south, while a larger trough dives down \nfrom the north. Out ahead of the first system, low (20-40%) rain \nchances develop once again across mainly the northern half of the \narea Sunday afternoon and evening. While the second system looks \ndrier overall, it will bring with it a front and gusty winds by \nMonday and into Tuesday. Just how strong winds become, and how cool \ntemperatures will be, depends on the track of the upper-level system \nand the strength of the front as it enters our area. Right now the \nNBM drops highs back into the 60s and low 70s by Tuesday and \nWednesday, but plenty of model spread still exists, so stay tuned \nfor updates! Otherwise, highs remain in the mid-to-upper 70s and low \n80s most days through the Long Term (upper 60s and low 70s Friday \nthanks to increased cloud cover and rain).\n\nSprang",
